A||||1||Patrick||Ogre, you might be a genius! (regarding "hot start" problem)
Z||000000||Patrick||04-16-99||07:26 PM||||<br>Ogre, I had to write this as soon as I got home.<p>I needed to take the Fiero through the emissions test today. This is always a cause for apprehension, because when I first got it two years ago, it failed miserably. Anyway, I took it for a good spin on the freeway to get the "cat" really hot, went through the test, and<b>passed</b> with flying colors. Hallelujah. So far, so good.<p>I then went to renew the insurance. I parked the car and handed a pile of money over to an insurance agent. Keep in mind now that this was the first time that I had turned off the car since I had gone for the buzz on the freeway. I should also mention that we set record temperatures here today for this time of year. It was 26 C, or for all you Americans, about 79 F.<p>Well, when I returned to the car, there was <b>NO</b> way it was going to start. I've had trouble with <b>hot starts</b> before, but I've never not been able to eventually get it going. The motor turned over fine, but it wouldn't even cough. Nothing. I waited another 15 minutes, tried it again, still no go. I didn't want to kill the battery, I wasn't sure what to do, except maybe to wait for it to cool down completely.<p>However, I remembered in another thread that you had suggested to me that the culprit might be the <b>MAP</b> sensor. Of course, I didn't have a spare one in my pocket, but I thought, hell, if I simply unplug the wiring harness from it, maybe the <b>ECM</b> will supply some kind of a <b>default</b> value. Hey, I was desperate! So I unplugged the harness, turned the ignition key and...I'm not kidding now...<b>It started IMMEDIATELY!!!!!!</b><p>I plugged the wiring harness back on, and off I went. There is obviously something wrong with this MAP sensor, and I will replace it, but I thought that someone else in a similar bind might benefit from your advice and my "innovative" procedure.<p>So thanks, Ogre. You were definitely on the ball with your initial suggestion.<br>   
Z||000001||theogre||04-17-99||02:19 PM||||your welcome 100%<p>there is nothing worse to fix, either in cars, or computers, than an intermitant problem.
